    <div class="left-column"></div>
    <div class="center-column">
      <h1>Arcadia ADL Introduction</h1>
      <p>
      This document introduces the Arcadia asset definition language, Arcadia ADL
      for short, the format in which the assets of Michael Heilmann's Arcadia are
      stored. ADL is defined by the <code>Arcadia.ADL</code> module: the module
      reads ADL assets, checks them against their schemata, and builds definitions
      that the Arcadia engine can consume.
      </p>

      <h2>What an ADL asset is</h2>
      <p>
      An ADL asset is a text file in the Arcadia Data Definition Language
      (<a href="@{siteAddress}/specifications/data-definition-language/">Arcadia DDL</a>).
      In the common case the file contains a single map that describes one
      definition. Every ADL definition carries at least two map entries:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>type</code>, a string that selects the kind of definition and the reader that processes it, and</li>
        <li><code>name</code>, a string that names the definition. Names are fully-qualified, dot-separated, and unique within an application, for example <code>Colors.Red</code> or <code>MainMenuScene.TextureColorModel</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
      The remaining entries are specific to the type. For example, the definition
      below describes the color red:
      </p>
      <pre>
{
  type : "Color",
  name : "Colors.Red",
  red : 255,
  green : 0,
  blue : 0,
}
      </pre>
      <p>
      Many definitions refer to other definitions. References are written as
      strings that name the referenced definition and are resolved only after all
      assets have been read. The following definitions, typical for a textured
      model, form a small example of this:
      </p>
      <pre>
{
  type : "PixelBuffer",
  name : "Assets.DefaultWall.PixelBuffer",
  width : 256,
  height : 256,
  operations : [ "Assets.DefaultWall.PixelBuffer.FillOperation" ],
}
{
  type : "PixelBufferOperations.CheckerboardFillOperation",
  name : "Assets.DefaultWall.PixelBuffer.FillOperation",
  checkerWidth : 32,
  checkerHeight : 32,
  firstCheckerColor : "Assets.DefaultWall.PixelBuffer.FillOperation.FirstColor",
  secondCheckerColor : "Assets.DefaultWall.PixelBuffer.FillOperation.SecondColor",
}
{
  type : "Texture",
  name : "Assets.DefaultWall.Texture",
  pixelBuffer : "Assets.DefaultWall.PixelBuffer",
}
      </pre>

      <h2>The two phases of loading</h2>
      <p>
      Loading ADL assets happens in two phases. In the first phase the application
      reads the assets into definitions; references between definitions are still
      unresolved at this point. In the second phase the application links the
      definitions, which resolves every reference and checks that the referenced
      definition has the required type.
      </p>
      <p>
      The example below reads the assets of the previous section from strings,
      links them, and fetches the texture definition:
      </p>
      <pre>
Arcadia_ADL_Context* context = Arcadia_ADL_Context_getOrCreate(thread);
Arcadia_ADL_Definitions* definitions = Arcadia_ADL_Definitions_create(thread);
// ... read each asset ...
Arcadia_ADL_Context_readFromString(thread, context, definitions,
  Arcadia_String_createFromCxxString(thread, textureAsset), Arcadia_BooleanValue_False);
Arcadia_ADL_Definitions_link(thread, definitions);
Arcadia_ADL_TextureDefinition* definition =
  (Arcadia_ADL_TextureDefinition*)Arcadia_ADL_Definitions_getDefinitionOrNull(
    thread, definitions, "Assets.DefaultWall.Texture");
      </pre>

      <h2>What else is in this documentation</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The <a href="@{siteAddress}/Arcadia/ADL/Concepts-and-Terms/">Concepts and Terms</a> document explains the ideas behind ADL: definitions, readers, schemata, references, and linking.</li>
        <li>The <a href="@{siteAddress}/Arcadia/ADL/Module-Interface/">Module Interface</a> document is the reference of the public interface of the <code>Arcadia.ADL</code> module.</li>
        <li>The <a href="@{siteAddress}/Arcadia/ADL/Specification/">Specification</a> document describes the ADL asset format in detail.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="right-column"></div>
  </main>
